---
import DocsNav from "../../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { architectureLanes, compilerStages, docs, repositoryUrl } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture | OrbitKV"
  description="OrbitKV compiler IR, physical design, block ownership, reclamation protocol, and NVIDIA backend boundaries."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
    <h1>Temporal physical design for model state.</h1>
    <p>
      OrbitKV compiles future-read semantics into block lifetime, address,
      immutable view, and reclamation programs.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Architecture" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Core contract</h2>
      <pre><code>{`Attention / state retention semantics
    -> LayoutProgram
       - SHA-256 plan fingerprint
       - address program
       - retirement program
       - minimum slot count
    -> KvBlockManager
       - request + class + ordinal identity
       - physical slot + generation
       - immutable KvView
       - semantic and execution frontiers
    -> RetirementCertificate
       - SemanticDead
       - ExecutionComplete
       - backend commit required before reuse`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Declarative Retention IR</h2>
      <p>
        The first frontend accepts an affine <code>may_read(query, key)</code>
        AST. Legacy Full/SWA JSON is desugared into the same relation before
        lifetime and address synthesis.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Rust HF-config frontend now derives explicit Full/SWA layer classes,
        KV bytes per token, Retention IR, and the final layout directly from a
        checkpoint <code>config.json</code>. Unknown explicit layer types fail
        closed; missing <code>layer_types</code> falls back to all-Full rather
        than guessing a finite lifetime.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`q - k < 1024
    -> proven q-k upper bound = 1023
    -> fixed window = 1024
    -> block death = block_end + 1023
    -> periodic cells = 65 at page size 16`}</code></pre>
      <pre><code>{`k < S OR q - k < W
    -> sink domain [0, S): pinned cells, never retired
    -> local domain [S, +inf): periodic cells, finite death
    -> no dedicated SinkSliding manager type`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Relations that do not prove a finite query-key distance safely lower
        to unbounded retention and append-only storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sink boundaries must align to reclamation pages. The generated address
        program rejects blocks outside each region. This normalization is
        exercised by the Rust simulator and owning manager; SGLang policy
        lowering still fails closed for partitioned domains.
      </p>

      <h2>Compiler stages</h2>
      <ol class="lifecycle-list">
        {compilerStages.map((stage) => (
          <li><span>{stage.key}</span><div><strong>{stage.name}</strong><p>{stage.detail}</p></div></li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <h2>Physical-plan optimization</h2>
      <p>
        The first engine optimizer consumes a compiled lifetime plan, per-rank
        KV budget, attention DP size, chunk size, admission target, and
        reclamation-call budget. It evaluates page-aligned SGLang SWA
        intervals, emits every cost and rejection reason, and selects a policy
        only inside an explicit runtime contract.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`16  -> reject: 5 reclamation calls > budget 4
32  -> select: 59,904 Full slots, 1 admission wave
64  -> feasible: 55,808 Full slots, 1 admission wave
128 -> reject: only 7 of 8 requests admitted`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The artifact records that the current lowering requires SWA ChunkCache
        with radix cache, overlap scheduling, speculative decoding, and
        disaggregation disabled. The SGLang plugin validates this contract and
        both pool capacities before using the plan.
      </p>

      <h2>Layer boundaries</h2>
      <div class="doc-grid">
        {architectureLanes.map((lane) => (
          <div class="doc-card">
            <span>{lane.id}</span>
            <h3>{lane.name}</h3>
            <p>{lane.detail}</p>
            <small>{lane.examples.join(" · ")}</small>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Why VMM is cost-gated</h2>
      <p>
        The recorded H20 requires a 2 MiB minimum CUDA VMM allocation. A small
        logical region can therefore incur severe rounding amplification.
        OrbitKV selects VMM only when stable virtual addresses are required and
        rounded bytes remain below the configured threshold.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`if !stable_address_required:
    Paged
elif !cuda_vmm_supported:
    Paged
elif rounded_vmm_bytes / logical_bytes > budget:
    Paged
else:
    CudaVmm`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Read the repository records</h2>
      <div class="doc-grid">
        {docs.map((item) => (
          <div class="doc-card">
            <span>{item.key}</span>
            <h3>{item.name}</h3>
            <p>{item.detail}</p>
            <a href={item.href}>Open record →</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Current qualification boundary.</strong>
        <p>
          The owning SGLang path supports SWA chunk cache with radix cache,
          speculative decoding, overlap scheduling, and CUDA Graph disabled.
          Unsupported combinations fail closed.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/h20-owning-vmm-validation-20260817.md`}>
          Full owning and VMM qualification
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
